WHAT YOU ALREADY KNOW   What you already know
You already know how to calculate a common-size percentage, a year-over-year change and a growth rate, and you know that Orontes's cost of goods sold has been growing faster than its sales. What you have not done yet is decide what anyone should do about it.
5.1  From a number to a finding
A finding has four parts, and an answer that leaves one out is incomplete.
It names the movement. Not "costs rose" but "cost of goods sold rose from 63.0 to 65.0 per cent of sales over two years".
It sizes the movement in money. Two percentage points of a $42,000,000 sales base is $840,000 a year. A manager cannot act on a percentage; he can act on a sum.
It is corroborated by a second figure. One number can be an error, a reclassification or an accident of timing. A finding stands when two independent figures point the same way.
It names where the cause must be. Not the cause itself — the statements rarely give that — but the part of the business in which the cause must lie. A falling gross margin is upstream of the factory gate: purchase prices, product mix, or discounting. It is not in selling and administration, because those sit below gross profit.
Figure F505-02 applies the four parts to Orontes.
Figure F505-02. One finding, in four parts
@table
Part | For Orontes
The movement | Cost of goods sold rose from 63.0 to 65.0 per cent of net sales, 2023 to 2025
Its size | About $840,000 a year at 2025 sales; $850,080 measured against sales growth
The corroboration | Gross profit grew at 11.43 per cent against sales at 14.56 per cent over the same period
Where the cause must be | Above gross profit: purchase prices, product mix, or selling price
@end
EXAM TRAP   A finding, not a calculation
An exam question that asks you to "analyse" wants the movement, its size, and what it implies — not the percentage alone.
Corroboration means a second figure that is not arithmetically derived from the first. Gross margin and cost of goods sold percentage are the same fact twice.
Never name a cause the statements do not support. Name the place the cause must be.
