SECTION CHECK   Test yourself
SC5-1  Which of these is a finding rather than a calculation?
A.	Inventory turnover is 11.5 times
B.	Receivables grew 10.2 per cent
C.	Receivables grew more slowly than sales, so collection has not deteriorated
D.	Total assets are $31,672,600
SC5-2  A falling gross profit margin places the cause:
A.	in selling and administrative expense.
B.	above gross profit: price, mix or purchase cost.
C.	in interest expense.
D.	in the tax charge.
SC5-3  Which pair of figures corroborates a finding, rather than restating it?
A.	Gross margin and cost of goods sold as a percentage of sales
B.	Net income and earnings per share
C.	A falling gross margin and a rise in the purchase price of sesame
D.	Total assets and total liabilities plus equity
Answers and explanations are at the end of the chapter.
5.2  The pattern in the cash
The largest single movement in Orontes's 2025 statements is cash, which rose by $5,202,100 to become 23.1 per cent of the company. Chapter 1 said this had to be explained before any liquidity ratio was believed. The statement of cash flows explains it.
Figure F505-03 is the summary.
Figure F505-03. Orontes Foods, Inc. — where the cash came from and went, 2025
@table
Source or use | Amount | Comment
Net cash provided by operating activities | 7,531,100 | Well above net income of 2,969,100
Purchases of property, plant and equipment | (3,000,000) | Replacing and extending the plant
Investment in Jordan Glass Co. | (1,200,000) | A stake in the bottle supplier
Net cash used in investing activities | (4,179,000) |
Issuance of common stock | 1,500,000 | New shares to existing owners
Proceeds from bank note | 800,000 |
Repayment of long-term debt | (400,000) |
Dividends paid | (50,000) | Against 450,000 declared
Net cash provided by financing activities | 1,850,000 |
Net increase in cash | 5,202,100 |
@end
Now the pattern can be stated. Operating cash flow of $7,531,100 against net income of $2,969,100 is two and a half times earnings, and the gap is the non-cash charges: depreciation of $2,100,000 and the impairment of $2,724,000. Those two alone are 2,100,000 + 2,724,000 = 4,824,000, which is most of the difference. The company earned far more cash than its income statement suggests, because a large part of its reported cost this year was a write-down of assets bought years ago.
Operating cash flow of $7,531,100 against capital expenditure of $3,000,000 leaves 7,531,100 − 3,000,000 = 4,531,100 of free cash flow. Orontes spent $1,200,000 of it on the Jordan Glass stake and $450,000 on dividends declared, and put most of the rest in the bank.
That is the finding. Orontes is not short of money and it is not hoarding for want of an alternative: it invested in plant and in a supplier during the year, and it still finished with a quarter of its balance sheet in cash. The question for a manager is why it also raised $1,500,000 of new equity and $800,000 of new debt in a year when it generated $7,531,100 from trading. A company does not normally raise money it does not need, and the reason will be in the board minutes rather than in the statements.
WORKED EXAMPLE   Worked example: free cash flow and what it says
Step 1. Free cash flow. Operating cash flow less capital expenditure: 7,531,100 − 3,000,000 = 4,531,100.
Step 2. Against dividends. Dividends declared were $450,000, so dividend cover on a cash basis is 4,531,100 ÷ 450,000 = 10.07 times. The dividend is not under threat on any view.
Step 3. Against debt. Total debt is the long-term portion plus the current portion: 6,000,000 + 400,000 = 6,400,000. One year of free cash flow would repay 4,531,100 ÷ 6,400,000 = 70.8 per cent of it.
Check. Three uses of one number, each answering a different stakeholder. The shareholder asks whether the dividend is safe; the lender asks how long repayment would take; the board asks what is left to invest. Chapter 10 formalises the second of these.
