A.	dividends.
B.	capital expenditure.
C.	interest.
D.	the increase in working capital.
SC5-6  Orontes generated $7,531,100 from operations and still raised $2,300,000 from shares and a bank note. The right response is to:
A.	conclude that the company is short of cash.
B.	conclude that the statements are inconsistent.
C.	ask why, because the statements do not say.
D.	treat the share issue as operating cash flow.
Answers and explanations are at the end of the chapter.
5.3  Patterns that cross the statements
The most reliable findings come from a movement in one statement that explains a movement in another. Figure F505-04 lists the pairs an analyst watches for.
Figure F505-04. Patterns that cross from one statement to another
@table
On the income statement | On the balance sheet | On the cash flow statement | What it means
Sales rising | Receivables rising faster | Operating cash flow flat or falling | Sales are being made on terms that are not being collected
Sales flat | Inventory rising | Operating cash flow falling | Goods are not selling and cash is going into stock
Profit rising | Payables rising sharply | Operating cash flow rising | Profit is being funded by suppliers, which cannot continue
Profit falling | Plant unchanged | Depreciation unchanged | Fixed costs are holding while revenue weakens
A large one-off charge | The written-down asset smaller | Operating cash flow unaffected | The charge is non-cash: earnings fell and cash did not
@end
The last row is Orontes exactly. The impairment of $2,724,000 cut operating income to $4,176,000 and left operating cash flow untouched at $7,531,100, because no money moved. An analyst who read only the income statement would record a bad year; an analyst who read only the cash flow statement would record an excellent one. Both are reading the same company.
The first row is Barada, from the case in Chapter 3. Sales up 11 per cent, receivables up 38 per cent and an overdraft where there was none: a classic pattern, and the one a credit officer is trained to see.
EXAM TRAP   Two statements beat one
A question that gives you both an income statement and a cash flow statement is usually testing whether you noticed they disagree.
Profit without cash is the pattern to be suspicious of. Cash without profit, as here, is usually a non-cash charge and is less alarming.
The balance sheet is where the difference between the two accumulates.
LANGUAGE FOCUS   "Quality" of earnings
English says earnings quality, and quality here does not mean good: it means how closely reported profit tracks cash and how likely it is to repeat. Arabic جودة الأرباح and French qualité des résultats carry the same technical sense. High-quality earnings may be small. Chapter 17 is about this.
