C.	the company is paying its suppliers faster.
D.	the payables balance is irrelevant to profit.
Answers and explanations are at the end of the chapter.
5.4  Four decisions a pattern can support
A finding is useful when someone can act on it. Figure F505-05 takes Orontes's four main patterns and names the decision each supports and the figure the decision turns on.
Figure F505-05. From pattern to decision
@table
Pattern | The decision it supports | The figure it turns on
Cost of goods sold growing faster than sales | Reprice, change mix, or renegotiate supply | $850,080 of gross profit forgone
Cash at 23.1 per cent of assets | Invest it, repay debt, or return it to owners | Free cash flow of $4,531,100
Equity at 64.4 per cent of assets | Borrow to fund the next expansion | Interest cover of 11.15 times
Impairment of goodwill and a brand | Review what else was bought at a premium | Goodwill down from 950,000 to 300,000
@end
Each row is a decision a board can take at its next meeting, and each one rests on a figure a reader of this book can compute. That is the standard an exam answer is held to at Level C: not a description of the statements, but a recommendation with a number behind it.
Two cautions belong here, and the exam tests both.
A pattern is evidence, not proof. The margin decline may be deliberate: a company buying market share prices below its old margin on purpose. The statements cannot distinguish a squeeze from a strategy, and an analyst says so.
A decision has a cost the statements do not show. Repricing loses customers. Returning cash to owners forgoes the next acquisition. The figures bound the decision; they do not make it.
WORKED EXAMPLE   Worked example: sizing a repricing decision
Step 1. The gap. Orontes's gross margin fell from 37.0 per cent in 2023 to 35.0 per cent in 2025, a loss of two percentage points.
Step 2. What recovering it is worth. Two points of the 2025 sales base: 42,000,000 × 0.02 = 840,000 of additional gross profit a year.
Step 3. What it would cost. To recover two points by price alone, Orontes must raise prices about 3.1 per cent, because a price rise adds to both sales and gross profit while cost of goods sold stays where it is. Whether the market accepts that is not in the statements.
Check. The analysis gives the board the size of the prize, $840,000, and the size of the price move needed, about 3 per cent. The decision about whether customers will pay it belongs to the people who sell to them.
