5.5  Writing it down
An analyst's output is a short piece of writing, and the exam's essay questions are marked on exactly the structure this chapter has described.
YOUR TURN   Build the finding
Figure F505-06 gives the four parts of a finding with two of them filled in. Complete the other two, for the pattern in Orontes's cash. Answers are at the end of the chapter.
Figure F505-06. Your turn: complete the finding (complete the blanks)
@table
Part | Entry
The movement | Cash rose from 7.9 to 23.1 per cent of total assets during 2025
Its size | ________
The corroboration | ________
Where the cause must be | Operating cash flow, which at 7,531,100 exceeded net income by two and a half times
@end
A good paragraph names the movement in its first sentence, sizes it in the second, corroborates it in the third and places the cause in the fourth. That is four sentences, and four sentences is usually enough. Candidates lose marks by describing the calculation instead of its consequence, and by naming a cause the statements do not support.
EXAM TRAP   What a written answer must contain
The movement, with the figures.
The size, in money.
A second figure that supports it.
Where the cause must lie, and what would settle it.
